<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Panama Has Delicious Food! – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 37.5%;"><b>Scenario:</b> Planning Meals for My Family</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 37.5%;"><b>Theme:</b> Panama Has Delicious Food!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Describe a family or school event they are looking forward to. Interactive: Exchange ideas about planning an event.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe a family event they are looking forward to (e.g., "I am happy to cook a healthy meal for my mom's birthday. I can prepare a salad, some rice, and meat."). Can exchange ideas about planning an event by collaborating with peers and making decisions together (e.g., "Let's plan a healthy meal for her birthday. What should we include?" "We can add rice and beans."). Can ask questions about details for planning (e.g., "What type of healthy Panamanian food can we use in our recipe?" "We need some rice and some beans.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by asking students to name their favorite Panamanian food. "What is your favorite Panamanian food? I like sancocho." Students respond in short sentences or phrases (e.g., "I like rice and beans," "Patacones are tasty."). The teacher shows flashcards of healthy Panamanian foods (e.g., mangos, watermelon, yuca, fresh fish) and asks, "Are these healthy? Yes, they are healthy!" This activates prior vocabulary and connects to the theme "Panama Has Delicious Food!".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences for planning a simple meal using target vocabulary and grammar. "I can cook rice and beans for lunch. We need some rice and some beans. First, I boil the rice. Next, I cook the beans." The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of /v/ in "vegetables" and /f/ in "fruit," and the suffix "-ing" in "cooking."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can we count every grain of rice? No, we can't. Rice is uncountable."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'cook' mean? Does it mean to eat raw food? No, it means to prepare food with heat."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario "Planning Meals for My Family".</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short dialogue script between two friends (Maria and Carlos) planning a healthy meal for a family picnic. The dialogue uses present simple, 'can', countable/uncountable nouns, and sequencing adverbs. The genre is an informal dialogue, focusing on expressing preferences and making simple suggestions. Register is conversational and friendly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will understand and extract information from a short spoken text about planning a meal and preparing to exchange ideas about planning an event.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher reads the dialogue aloud twice. Students listen for specific information.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What are Maria and Carlos planning?" (Answer: A healthy meal for a picnic.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False statements:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Maria wants to bring some juice." (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Carlos likes salty snacks." (False, he likes healthy fruits.)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"They can cook some rice and beans." (True)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice using present simple for preferences, modal 'can' for suggestions, countable/uncountable nouns with 'some' and 'many', and sequencing adverbs 'first', 'next'. Focus on accurate pronunciation of /v/ and /f/ and the suffix '-ing'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion:</b> The teacher writes incomplete sentences on the board related to the dialogue or meal planning. Students orally complete them, focusing on target grammar and vocabulary. Example: "I like to eat _______ for breakfast." (Possible answers: bread, mangos, fruit). "We ______ cook a healthy meal." (Answer: can). "We need ______ water and ______ tomatoes." (Answers: some, many). The teacher ensures correct pronunciation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice - Picture Cue Drill (Pair work):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher shows flashcards of food items (e.g., "rice", "vegetables", "juice", "chicken"). One student asks, "What can we cook?" The other student responds, "We can cook some rice and some vegetables." or "We need some juice." They swap roles. The teacher circulates, providing feedback on pronunciation, especially /v/ and /f/, and correct use of 'some'/'many'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Guided Pair Interaction - Asking Preferences:</b> Students stay in pairs. They use simple sentence frames to ask and answer about food preferences and quantities.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "What do you like to eat for lunch?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "I like to eat some rice and beans. I often eat fresh fruit."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "What do you need for a healthy snack?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "I need some mangos or some watermelon."</li>
                        </ul>
                        The teacher encourages using adjectives like 'tasty', 'healthy', 'fresh'.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Planning Meals for My Family".</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Role-play "Let's Plan a Healthy Meal for a Family Birthday!"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in groups of three. Their task is to plan a healthy Panamanian meal for a family birthday celebration. They must decide what to cook, what ingredients they need, and assign roles for preparing the meal. They should aim to use all target grammar (present simple, 'can', countable/uncountable nouns with 'some'/'many') and vocabulary (meal times, food items, verbs like 'cook', 'cut', 'slice', adjectives like 'healthy', 'tasty', and sequencing adverbs 'first', 'next'). The teacher provides a simple planning sheet with prompts (e.g., "Event:", "Main dish:", "Side dishes:", "Drinks:", "Who cooks what:").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Dialogue Snippets:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student 1: "Let's plan a healthy meal for mom's birthday! What should we cook?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student 2: "We can cook some rice and beans. They are tasty!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student 3: "Yes! And we can add some fresh vegetables like lettuce and tomatoes for a salad."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student 1: "Good idea! So, first, we need some rice. Next, we buy many tomatoes. What about drinks?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student 2: "We can make some natural juice, like mango juice. It's very sweet."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student 3: "I can cut the vegetables. Who can boil the rice?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">After planning, each group verbally presents their meal plan to another group or the whole class, explaining what they decided to cook and why it's healthy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the performance task.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their planned healthy meal for the family birthday. Each group briefly describes their meal, the ingredients, and who does what. The teacher listens for their oral performance.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially /v/ in "vegetables", /f/ in "fruit").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., rice, beans, mangos, vegetables, cook, tasty, healthy).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of grammar (present simple, 'can', countable/uncountable nouns with 'some'/'many').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of ideas and coherent description of the meal plan.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborative interaction within the group (exchanging ideas, asking questions).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a small piece of paper before leaving.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I can describe ____________________." (e.g., healthy food, a meal I like).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can plan a meal with ____________________." (e.g., a friend, my family).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy for me today when talking about food?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new word or phrase I can use to plan a meal?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in terms of student engagement and speaking practice? (e.g., "The role-play was effective, students actively used 'can' and 'some/many'.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., "Some students struggled with sequencing adverbs; I need to reinforce 'first, next' with more visual cues. Also, pronunciation of '/v/' still needs more practice.")</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> "Great job speaking about our meal plans today! Tomorrow, we will practice writing down our healthy meal plans and invitations for our family events, focusing on correct sentence structure and spelling of our new food words."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>